Date: Wed, 22 Dec 1993 10:20:08 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Bayes, Dropouts, and Geniuses

As a "drop out" myself, let me comment on the point Ray Cromwell makes:

> (BTW: I'm not talking about people who drop out of anything, but
> people who drop out of school, particularly high school. How many
> supergeniuses are illiterate, or dropped out in the 9th grade, etc)
..
>    While Bill Gates was a drop out, and he is visionary in some respects,
> I would not place the success of microsoft solely on his back but also
> on the thousands of computer scientists and engineers which he employs --
> people who didn't drop out.  Gate's is certainly good at management, but
> as far as programming/analysis goes, he's no Donald Knuth.

Calling Bill Gates a "drop out" is mostly a trivialization of the term
"drop out," which is perhaps what Ray was getting in.

Gates graduated from high school, but chose to only attend Harvard for
one or two years (not sure which) before joining Paul Allen to start
up Micrsoft. I would hardly call this the conventional definition of
"drop out."

Nor would I call a bright person who failed to graduate from some
particular high school a "drop out" in the negative sense used to
discuss the drug addicts, losers, and drop outs we were initially
discussing.

Of course, I don't know what others consider drop outs to be. Speaking
for myself, I mean all the folks who cruised through school, possibly
never read a book for their own pleasure, lived for their drunken
parties, and now find themselves largely unemployable--desptite what
Jeff Fabijanic claimed about all the drop outs he knows who are now
race car drivers (there must be _millions_ of those), rocket
scientists, authors, and the like. I suspect we're using the term
"drop out" in radically different ways.

In any case, those drop outs that succeed as race car drivers and chip
designers, Jeff's friends, clearly need no subsidies stolen from me.
Hence no need for welfare, AFDC, "substance abuse" programs, etc.

But about the truly clueless, who have no skills others wish to
compensate them for, my earlier comments stand: "No work, no eat."

--Tim May, who didn't go to grad school but instead went into
industry, and who is thus a "drop out" when compared to those who did
go to grad school....it's all relative




