Date: Wed, 22 Dec 1993 12:09:38 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Extropian Outreach?

I want to expand on what I think is an important point:

> For those of you who want to convert nonLibertarians, please go right
> ahead and do so. Either here (though I didn't notice Mark Venture
> answering any of Betsy's, Nancy's, or Amara's questions....) or in the
> many "outreach" programs the LP and SIL organizations sponsor.
> 
> "Outreach" is the interesting word here. People stumble across this
> list for various reasons. Are we to hide our lights under a bushel, as
> so many women are (wrongly) taught to do, or are we to openly and
> defiantly to proclaim our views?

Should Extropians be concerned about outreach? Certainly Max would
like more subscribers to his magazine, so as to break even, let alone
to expand the pool of Extropians. And certainly we need some more
thinkers on this list, having lost several of our core thinkers in the
last half-year or so.

But I suspect we are too small a group to do effective outreach. And I
notice very little talk of this....the various luncheons, parties,
Extropoganzas, and perhaps even the "Extro 1" conference, are not
really oriented toward roping in new converts and giving them a
"palatable" version of Extropianism.

Unlike the Moonies (recall Duncan's tale) or other groups, which often
practice strategic deception about their ultimate views (so as to get
past the initial "sales resistance" of newcomers and skeptics), the
Extropians have always been an elitist, "in-your-face" sort of group.
We freely speculated about Jupiter-sized brains, the Singularity,
uploading, living forever, undermining governments, and on and on,
with few worries that this will frighten the horses and reduce our
growth rate.

Frankly, I doubt Max will ever get more than a few thousand
subscribers. Further, I have yet to see any evidence that people who
discover "Extropy" on the newstand, for example, are then joining this
list and becoming core contributors. Are there any of you out there?
(No offense to the lurkers, but I don't mean "are there _any_ of you
out there who discovered us throught the magazine.")

But this issue of outreach, of whether a goal is to recruit more
Extropians, is something that sorely needs to be debated, somewhere.

Unfortunately, few of the "Board" members appear to be reading the
List, let alone contributing to the discussion, so I suspect any
debate we have here will be nearly meaningless.

Personally, I'm not much interested in adding a few thousand new
Extropians to the world, except insofar as it makes the discussion
here on this list more interesting and the wild parties in the Bay
Area even more interesting.

--Tim May


