Date: Wed, 22 Dec 93 16:16:55 -0500
From: hhuang@MIT.EDU
Subject: POLI/LEGAL: discrimination laws

Ray Cromwell writes,

>A few weeks ago, our campus newspaper was stolen because of
>"insensitive" comments about the Reginald Denney trial. I'm so used to
>people exploding over comments far less strong than Tim's that I
>expect it and try to plan for it. Hopefully, I can go back to normal
>after I leave college.

Forget college.  Discrimination laws have already brought political
correctness to the workplaces of America.  As I'll demonstrate, things
will continue to worsen until the entire nonsense is stopped.

It begins with Title VII of the 1964 Civil Rights Act, the act that
many American liberals are so proud of.  Title VII held it to be,

  an unlawful employment practice for an employer ... to discriminate
  against any individual with respect to his compensation, terms,
  conditions, or privileges of employment, because of such individual's
  race, color, religion, sex, or national origin.

Under this was created the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission
(EEOC).  In 1980, drawing upon a body of burgeoning sexual harassment
caselaw, the EEOC put forth its guidelines on sexual harassment that
proscribed ``unwelcome sexual advances, requests for sexual favors,
and other verbal or physical conduct of a sexual nature'' and other
such conduct that ``has the purpose or effect of unreasonably
interfering with an individual's work performance or creating an
intimidating, hostile, or offensive working environment'' as
actionable as sex discrimination under Title VII.

The controversy over sexual harassment finally led to the 1986
_Meritor Savings Bank v. Vinson_ Supreme Court decision which
reaffirmed that,

  Held: A claim of ``hostile environment'' sexual harassment is a form
  of sex discrimination that is actionable under Title VII.  The
  language of Title VII is not limited to ``economic'' or ``tangible''
  discrimination.  [EEOC] guidelines fully support the view that
  sexual harassment leading to noneconomic injury can violate Title
  VII.

This is an excellent example of rights expansion.  First, one receives
the fictitious ``right'' to be free from discrimination.  Then, that
right expands to include the right to be free from sexual
``harassment'': a ``hostile environment'' which need not even be
economic or tangible in basis.

However, the Court was aware of the potential dangers, so it said that:

  ``[M]ere utterance of an ethnic or racial epithet which engenders
  offensive feelings in an employee'' would not affect the conditions
  of employment to sufficiently significant degree to violate Title
  VII. ...  For sexual harassment to be actionable, it must be
  sufficiently severe or pervasive ``to alter the conditions of [the
  victim's] employment and create an abusive working environment.''

But who decides what is ``sufficiently severe or pervasive''?  Ray
notes that he's gotten used to people exploding over the merest
slights, real or imagined.  Worse, people are now told that they must
further increase their sensitivity as a means of liberating
themselves!

In 1991, Congress gave sexual harassment victims the right to a jury
trial and the right to sue for back pay compensation.  The problem is
that while juries are understandably hesitant about condemning alleged
harassers to jail sentences, juries have much less trouble charging
companies with monetary damages.  And unlike many criminal cases,
where a ``smoking gun'' needs to be found, a company can be guilty by
not taking sufficient steps to curb ``hostile environment.''  Merely
doing nothing may not be good enough in court (``Do you feel
lucky?'').  One is not so much *prohibited* from engaging in sexual
harassing behavior so much as one is *obligated* to do something about
it, or else.  Many companies, finding themselves increasingly at risk
to ligitation, especially after the Hill-Thomas hearings, began
curbing the behavior of their employees -- de facto behavior and
speech codes.

But that a government standard would result in a chilling effect on
speech, should this not be in violation of the First Amendment?  Yes,
if certain precedents are any indication.  But the Supreme Court has
in general been averse to dealing with politically divisive issues,
issues such as abortion and sexual harassment.  In the recent 1993
_Harris v. Forklift Systems_ case, the Court did little more than
weaken slightly the conditions of sexual harassment, and in a speedy
decision, sent the case back down without ruling on it one way or the
other.  The First Amendment was mentioned nowhere in the decision:

  Held: To be actionable as ``abusive work environment'' harassment,
  conduct need not ``seriously affect [an employee's] psychological
  well-being'' or lead the plaintiff to ``suffe[r] injury.''

  ...

  This standard, which we reaffirm today, takes a middle path between
  making actionable any conduct that is merely offensive and requiring
  the conduct to cause a tangible psychological injury. ...  Conduct
  that is not severe or pervasive enough to create an objectively
  hostile or abusive work environment -- an environment that a
  reasonable person would find hostile or abusive -- is beyond Title
  VII's purview.

But once again, who decides what a ``resonable person would find
hostile or abusive''?

Now observe rights expansion along a different front: the growth in
the number of categories protected from discrimination as a result of
the 1992 Americans with Disabilities Act, which George Bush bragged at
the 1992 GOP Convention about passing.  So diability is protected from
workplace discrimination.  But who decides what a disability is, if it
is based upon the duration, severity, and difficulty of changing the
said condition?

In July 1993, regarding a Rhode Island district case, the EEOC filed a
court brief to the First Circuit Appeals declaring extreme obesity a
disability, thereby opening the door to obesity discrimination suits.
Amazingly, the EEOC insisted in its brief that,

  although it is possible for an obese individual to lose weight,
  obesity is a chronic, lifelong condition. ... It is not necessary
  that a condition be involuntary or immutable to be covered.

The plaintiff's counsel was provided for by, of course, the ACLU.  In
November 1993, a Boston-based three judge appellate panel ruled in
favor of the plaintiff citing that,

  In a society that all too often confuses ``slim'' with ``beautiful''
  or ``good,'' morbid obesity can present formidable barriers to
  employment.

So it seems that the disabilities act can cover even voluntary or
mutable conditions that can somehow still be deemed as ``chronic'' or
``lifelong.''  (Confused?  You should be.)  Alcoholism, for example,
is protected under disability law.  What next?

I must mention yet another protected category, age, as covered by the
Age Disabilities Act of 1967.  One must be at least 40 years of age to
sue for compensation, which can include both back pay as well as
``front pay'': the damage a person suffers because his or her age
makes it difficult to easily get another job.  These cases can be
easier to prove than race or sex based cases, because the employer
need not be shown to be racist or sexist.  Older people are frequently
in higher salaried positions and so are often more likely to be laid
off during company restructurings.  Who can even say if this is age
bias, or just smart business decision-making?  But government knows
best.  A recent study by Jury Verdict Research showed that age
discrimination suits on average win the biggest jury awards.

A major result of anti-discrimination laws is de facto quotas.  Even
assuming random hiring, probabilistically there will necessarily exist
companies that will have seemingly ``racist'' hiring patterns.  Since
one is always potentially liable on charges of race discrimination,
the question is, ``Do you feel lucky?''  So many employers quietly
bias their hiring patterns along politically correct lines -- de facto
quotas.  Also, we are always told how minorities are less financially
well off, live in worse neighborhoods, and receive poorer educations.
So should employers happen to hire less people of those minorities,
who can say that this is racist, or just good business sense?  But
government knows best.  And what about ``affirmative action'' in
schools admissions and in government employment?  Isn't that illegal
discrimination on the basis of race, sex, et. al.?  Silence.

And note the recent fuss with banks being accused of ``racism''
because many blacks find it harder than whites to get loans.  People
are upset about this, and once again, government is called upon to
``do something'' about it.  In the meantime, banks are guilty until
proven innocent.

In summary, discrimination legislation and jurisprudence will intrude
more and more upon private property and freedom of association rights.
So, Ray, though I'd like to think that once one gets out of college,
that things will get better, I'll note that government is growing
increasingly intrusive on the discrimination and harassment fronts.

For example, on October 1, 1993, the EEOC put forth trial balloons in
the form of proposed new guidelines.  The comment period on these
ended November 30.  In a _Wall Street Journal_ column, ``After
_Harris_, More Questions on Harassment,'' Ms. R. Gaull Silberman, vice
chairman of the EEOC in Washington, writes that there are,

  important differences between the EEOC's proposed guidelines, which
  cover race, color, national origin, gender, age, and disability, and
  the existing sexual harassment guidelines. ...  Under the proposed
  guidelines, harassing conduct including ``epithets, slurs, negative
  stereotyping, or threatening, intimidating, or hostile acts'' and
  ``written or graphic material that denigrates or shows hostility or
  aversion toward an individual or group because of (a prohibited
  basis)'' would establish a hostile environment.

Incredibly, the EEOC proposed to expand the hostile environment clause
to include ``epithets, slurs, negative stereotyping'' and to greatly
expand the number of categories covered under harassment to include
all protected categories, not just gender.  And if the judicial record
is any evidence, courts have continually reaffirmed EEOC guidelines.

We will soon find out what the new EEOC guidelines will be; perhaps
the First Amendment criticisms the EEOC has received will convince it
to deviate from its original plan.

But even if the EEOC is deterred this time, the numbers of newly
PC-indoctrinated lawyers, judges, and politicians continue to
increase.  If you think about it, political correctness, legally
speaking, is almost entirely based on discrimination laws -- bans on
improper thoughts and actions regarding minorities.  Most importantly,
many people, lacking a moral defense, cave in on these issues (``Are
you an X-ist?  How dare you oppose anti-X-ism laws!'').  So until this
nonsense is stopped, things will only get worse.

On freedom, the analogy is frequently made that if you want to boil a
live frog, you put it in cold water, and then slowly heat the water to
a boil.  That is exactly what is happening here: ``But there are too
many legal precedents now to go back.''

Final remarks:

PC doctrine tells us that in order to liberate ourselves from
oppression, one of the things we must do is to become supersensitized
to all the possible things that might hurt or offend one any
particular group.  Hence, for example, Connecticut's imposition of
mandatory gender sensitivity training workshops for all sufficiently
large workplaces.  (At one point, a similar bill passed one house and
nearly passed the other house of the Massachusetts legislature as
well.)

But, really, can it possibly be ``liberating'' to enslave one's
emotions to react to every small bit of perceived offense?  How can
such behavior be at all rewarding?  Well, in a free market, my guess
is that it probably wouldn't be (nobody can say for sure).  However
these days, given the increasing number of and damage awarded in
discrimination and harassment suits, and that the guiltier one can
make society feel, the more one can wring concessions for one's group,
it's the squeaky wheel that gets the grease.

Everything that lies wrong with socialism is that unproductive
behaviors -- such as hypersensitivity to offense and the blaming of
everything on discriminatory oppression -- get rewarded.  And by
robbing from the productive, no less.

-Han Y. Huang                                           hhuang@mit.edu

